    "description": "Mobile gaming in Iran connects everyday play with local Android marketplaces and internationally distributed games. Historical reporting and individual game listings illuminate this ecosystem, although they do not establish its current national size.",
    "tags": ["Mobile gaming", "Android", "Digital distribution", "Iranian games", "Game development"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:57:37Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:57:37Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Mobile Games in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s mobile gaming ecosystem, local Android marketplaces, online strategy games, and the limits of available market evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "mobile-play",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A platform for everyday entertainment",
        "content": "Mobile games belong to both the consumption and production sides of Iranian digital culture: people play imported and locally distributed titles, while developers also create games for international audiences. The available evidence captures different parts of that story. A historical report describes children’s use of mobile devices and local marketplaces; a developer interview explains a studio’s move into mobile production; and a contemporary storefront listing shows how an online strategy game presents itself to players. These sources should be read at their own scale rather than combined into a national market estimate. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "distribution",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Local stores and different audiences",
        "content": "Xinhua’s November 2018 account of research attributed to Iran’s Digital Games Research Center, known as DIREC, identified Café Bazaar as an important Android game download channel for Iranian children. It also named Myket, Iran Apps, and Google Play. The same report associated earlier exposure to games in major urban areas with greater access to smartphones, tablets, and computers. This is useful historical evidence of differences in access, but it does not establish today’s distribution shares or a current urban–rural divide. [s1]\n\nThe report described children playing runner, arcade, driving, puzzle, and strategy games, with freemium play prominent. Freemium means that initial access is free while additional features or goods may require payment. That distinction matters when interpreting audience figures: downloading or playing a game does not necessarily mean purchasing it. The underlying survey questionnaire and sampling documentation were not inspected, so its findings are presented here as attributed historical reporting. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "game-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What an online mobile game involves",
        "content": "Pesarkhandeh, listed on Google Play under Paeezan, provides a concrete example of an online strategy format. Its developer-written description emphasizes turn-based battles, collectible heroes, alliances, rankings, and seasonal events. These features describe an experience organized around recurring participation and encounters with other players, rather than a self-contained story that ends after a campaign. The listing also identifies advertising and user interaction. [s2]\n\nA storefront is evidence of how a product is offered and described, not an independent audit of its performance. Its download badge cannot establish Iranian active users, and its promotional references to worldwide competition do not measure the geographic composition of its audience. Likewise, developer-provided privacy declarations should remain distinguishable from independently tested privacy findings. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "production",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Mobile production and creative scope",
        "content": "In a published interview, Dead Mage director Amir Fassihi described Shadow Blade as a move toward a smaller action-platforming project after the more demanding development of Garshasp. His account illustrates how mobile development can involve adapting existing production skills to a more manageable project. It is one developer’s experience, rather than a model that explains every Iranian studio. [s3]\n\nTogether, these examples suggest useful questions for exploring the field: where a game is distributed, whom it serves, how it earns money, and how much ongoing support it requires. They also show why mobile gaming should not be reduced to a single popularity ranking. A stronger current overview requires independently documented audience research and comparable platform reports.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
