    "description": "Iran-connected game development includes work drawing on Persian mythology and projects created for international audiences. Studio interviews and publishing records reveal how creative production, project management, and commercial distribution involve distinct responsibilities.",
    "tags": ["Game studios", "Game development", "Independent games", "Dead Mage", "Publishing", "Persian mythology"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:57:37Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:57:37Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran-Connected Game Studios | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore game development through Persian mythology, Dead Mage’s production experience, and the distinct roles of studios and publishers."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A studio is a production team",
        "content": "A game studio brings together the people and processes needed to make an interactive work. In a published interview, Dead Mage director Amir Fassihi described responsibilities extending beyond programming to artists’ priorities, design staffing, music, hiring, and release planning. His account makes the studio visible as an organization that sustains creative work, rather than simply a brand attached to a finished game. [s1]\n\n“Iran-connected” requires care as a description. Fassihi identifies himself as Iranian-born, while the interview introduces him as based in Vancouver. A creator’s background, a team’s working location, and a company’s legal home are different facts. This page follows documented creative connections without treating them as proof that every associated business or employee is currently located inside Iran. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mythology",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Persian mythology as playable material",
        "content": "Garshasp: The Monster Slayer offers a concrete encounter with Persian mythological material. Its Steam listing credits Dead Mage as developer and Digital Dragon as publisher, with a release date of May 9, 2011. The game presents Garshasp as a hero seeking an ancestral mace taken by monstrous opponents called Deevs. Its stated mechanics combine close combat, platforming, and exploration. [s2]\n\nFor readers unfamiliar with these traditions, the key distinction is between adaptation and historical reconstruction. The store explicitly presents a mythological fantasy world. Its characters and conflicts are evidence of how the game uses literary and legendary material, rather than documentation of events in ancient Iran. The listing also records Persian alongside other supported languages, showing that an internationally distributed product can retain a Persian-language presentation. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Learning to choose a workable project",
        "content": "Fassihi’s account emphasizes project scope: the amount of content and production effort a team attempts. He describes Garshasp as an overly ambitious early undertaking and Shadow Blade as a smaller mobile project better suited to the team. He also discusses the need for a distinctive creative hook when marketing resources are limited. These are a practitioner’s reflections, not independently measured findings about the entire industry. They nevertheless explain why studio development involves choosing what a team can finish and polish. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "publishing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Making a game and bringing it to market",
        "content": "Development and publishing credits answer different questions. The Garshasp listing separates the developer from the publisher; Medrick’s corporate description, by contrast, identifies its business as combining development with publishing. Medrick lists services including marketing, customer support, technical infrastructure, and localization. Localization adapts a product for another language or audience, while those other services address discovery, operation, and player support. Its description establishes what the company says it offers, rather than independently verifying the quality or reach of those services. [s2][s3]\n\nThese examples provide a useful way to explore Iran-connected studios: follow a specific game, inspect its credits, and distinguish creative contributions from publishing services. A national directory requires additional work on ownership, location, active projects, and employment. International visibility alone cannot reveal how much production took place within Iran.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
